Friday, December 12, midday. Desmond spent the morning on the compost bin, and I want to keep a record of what he found because it will matter when he compares winters. The core read 112°F on his long probe thermometer, confirmed against an ice-water check that gave 33°F, so the probe is trustworthy to within a degree. The bin is the three-foot cube he built from pallet boards, and the outer foot is frozen. I suggested the old 60 by 80 inch wool blanket with 6-mil plastic over it, and I put a Sunday check on the list to see whether the core holds above 90°F. If it falls below that, the likely cause is too little nitrogen, and the remedy would be to bury a bucket of kitchen scraps in the center rather than turning the whole pile in the cold. He explained, as he likes to do, that he turns the pile only when the core passes 140°F in summer, and never in deep frost, because he does not want to chill the heat he has made. That is a sound habit and I will not argue with it. I should remember that his instinct is to protect the core first and mix later.

The second matter was a pan. He had assumed his half-sheet pan was 12 by 17 inches and measured it to be 13 by 18. I recorded the corrected size next to the granola formula so that future quantity scaling for sheet-pan recipes uses the right area, about 234 square inches. That affects how deep the oats lie and how fast they dry, so it is worth having correct. I will use it when scaling roasted vegetables as well.

Open items: ask Sunday evening about the granola crunch, and lift the blanket to check the core temperature.On the next visit I should also ask whether the plastic sheet is holding against wind.}, {
